---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Cmd from '../components/Cmd.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import { faqPick, site } from '../content/site';
import { mdInline } from '../content/readme';

const title = 'Claude Code memory between sessions | hippo-memory';
const description =
  'Persistent memory for Claude Code. hippo init adds hooks that keep pinned memories in context, save state before compaction and store failed tool calls.';

// README "Framework Integrations": what `hippo init` adds to Claude Code's settings.
const hooks = [
  { event: 'SessionStart', does: "Prints what the previous session's `hippo sleep` consolidated." },
  { event: 'UserPromptSubmit', does: 'Keeps your pinned memories and the last 5 writes in context. An unchanged block is skipped, and sent again after 10 skips or a compaction.' },
  { event: 'PreCompact', does: 'Saves a snapshot of the task, a summary and the next step before the transcript is summarized.' },
  { event: 'SessionStart (compact)', does: 'Prints that snapshot and the recent session trail back into context after compaction.' },
  { event: 'PostCompact', does: 'Tells you what was saved, and prints nothing when nothing was.' },
  { event: 'PostToolUseFailure', does: '`hippo capture-error` stores a failed tool call as an error memory. It skips interrupts, declined permissions and searches that found nothing, and stores a repeated failure once.' },
  { event: 'SessionEnd', does: "Runs `hippo sleep`, which consolidates the store and imports new entries from the project's Claude Code auto memory, then `hippo capture` on the session transcript." },
];

const plugin = `${site.links.repo}/tree/master/extensions/claude-code-plugin`;
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Claude Code" published="2026-09-28">
  <Nav />
  <main id="main">
    <PageIntro eyebrow="Claude Code">
      <Fragment slot="title">Claude Code memory <span class="text-gradient">between sessions</span></Fragment>
      <p>
        Claude Code loads two things into every session: the <code>CLAUDE.md</code> files you write and the auto memory
        notes it writes itself. Hippo adds a store of lessons that carries over between sessions: what failed, what
        fixed it, and which facts changed.
      </p>
      <p>
        It recalls the lessons that match the task, keeps pinned ones in context, ranks the ones you mark wrong lower,
        and leaves out the ones you replace. The store is SQLite in your project, with markdown mirrors you can read and commit.
      </p>
    </PageIntro>

    <Section title="Set it up in two commands">
      <Cmd lines={[site.installCmd, 'hippo init', '# optional: register every git repo under ~ at once', site.initCmd]} />
      <p>
        If the project has a <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>hippo init</code> adds a short block telling Claude to run
        <code>hippo context --auto</code> when a session starts, and adds the hooks below to Claude Code's settings. It
        also installs one daily run at 6:15am that learns from the day's commits in every registered project.
        <code>hippo init --no-hooks --no-schedule</code> skips both, and <code>hippo doctor</code> checks the install.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="What the hooks do" alt>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-white/8">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[520px] text-left text-sm">
          <caption class="sr-only">Claude Code hooks that hippo init installs</caption>
          <thead class="bg-white/[0.03] font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th scope="col" class="px-5 py-3 font-medium">Hook</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-5 py-3 font-medium">What it does</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
            {hooks.map((h) => (
              <tr class="align-top">
                <th scope="row" class="whitespace-nowrap px-5 py-3 text-left font-mono text-xs font-normal text-acc-cyan">{h.event}</th>
                <td class="copy px-5 py-3 text-zinc-300" set:html={mdInline(h.does)} />
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The <a class="link" href={plugin} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Claude Code plugin<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a>
        is the alternative to these hooks; use one, not both. <code>hippo hook uninstall claude-code</code> removes the hooks.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A mistakes file that maintains itself">
      <p>
        Some developers ask Claude Code to keep a <code>MISTAKES.md</code> file of what went wrong. Hippo keeps that record
        for you. The <code>PostToolUseFailure</code> hook turns failed tool calls into error memories, and error memories
        get twice the half-life of ordinary ones. You can add, correct and replace lessons yourself:
      </p>
      <Cmd
        lines={[
          'hippo remember "billing uses pnpm; never run npm install here" --error',
          '# a recalled lesson was wrong:',
          'hippo outcome --bad',
          '# a fact changed:',
          'hippo supersede mem_a1b2c3 "billing moved to bun"',
          '# what the failure hook saw:',
          'hippo failures',
        ]}
      />
      <p>
        Recall pulls the lessons that match the task, ranked by relevance, strength and recency, and a lesson marked wrong
        drops out of the top results.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Memories you can read and commit" alt>
      <p>
        The store is SQLite: <code>.hippo/hippo.db</code> in each project, plus <code>~/.hippo/</code> for lessons shared
        across projects. After each change hippo writes markdown mirrors beside it, so you can read, grep and commit what
        your agent has learned. The same store serves Codex, Cursor and any MCP client, so a lesson Claude Code learned is
        there when you switch tools.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="MCP, and where CLAUDE.md fits">
      <p>
        <code>claude mcp add hippo-memory -- hippo mcp</code> gives Claude Code hippo's memory tools over MCP; the
        <a class="link" href="/mcp/">MCP server page</a> lists them. Keep <code>CLAUDE.md</code> for short standing rules:
        <a class="link" href="/vs/claude-memory/">CLAUDE.md vs memory</a> covers what goes where, and
        <code>hippo import --claude CLAUDE.md</code> turns notes that piled up there into memories.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Faq
      items={faqPick(['How do I give Claude Code memory between sessions?', 'Do I still need CLAUDE.md?', 'What happens when a memory turns out to be wrong?'])}
      heading="Claude Code questions"
      moreHref="/faq/"
    />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
